#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each cell is the weight of one score pattern of the item pair in two-level "
"Guttman errors (Koopman et al., 2017): 0 for patterns consistent with the "
"item-step ordering, larger for more serious errors. Ties in item-step "
"popularity are averaged over the possible orderings."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each column shows the partition obtained with one lower bound c; each cell "
"is the scale an item joins (0 = unscalable)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each decision below is explained for the option currently selected, with "
"guidance on when an alternative would fit your data better."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each row is a cluster of items with a tied position in the fixed order; the "
"statistics check whether the clusters keep their order for every rest-score "
"group."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each row is one data set completed by two-way imputation with a different "
"random error. Every other table of this analysis comes from the first set "
"only; this table is a sensitivity check, not pooled multiple-imputation "
"inference: no combination rule is applied, because their validity for Mokken "
"statistics has not been established."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"Each step removes the item with the worst local-dependence flags and checks "
"the rest again (Straat et al., 2016). Kept: items in the final set, where "
"the procedure flags no further local dependence; a stepwise procedure does "
"not guarantee the largest such set."
msgstr ""

#: package/datasets/TestforReceptionofGrammarTROG(n=210).description
msgid ""
"Eighty dichotomous items of the Norwegian TROG in 20 clusters of four items "
"(a1-a4 ... t1-t4), the clustered-item example of Koopman and Braeken for "
"investigating the ordering structure of a test, from the mokken package - "
"see data/README.md"
msgstr ""

