#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The attribute is unobserved, so it is replaced by the rest score: the total "
"score on the other items. Respondents are grouped by rest score and, for "
"each item step, the proportion P(X ≥ k) is compared across groups. Every "
"decrease larger than the minimum violation (#vi) is tested; #zsig counts the "
"significant ones."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The backward selection removed %d item(s), in this order: %s. With them "
"removed, the procedure detects no further violations under these settings; "
"that is not proof that the remaining items have an invariant ordering."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The bootstrap could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The bootstrap repeats the selection, with the same options, on many samples "
"drawn with replacement from your respondents (whole clusters, with nested "
"data), the way a new sample of the same population might look."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The check could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The coefficients could not be computed by group: %s. Each group needs enough "
"cases with variation on every item."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The correlation bounds could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The declared score range (%s to %s) does not contain every observed score "
"(observed range %s to %s), or its lowest value is not below its highest. "
"Correct the declared range, or use the range observed in the data."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The fences are outlier thresholds. Tukey's fence (U1) assumes a roughly "
"symmetric distribution; error counts are usually skewed, so the "
"medcouple-adjusted fence (U2) is the better guide."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The first scale starts from the items you chose instead of from the pair "
"with the highest Hij. Use it to build a scale around items that define the "
"attribute theoretically, and check that the result does not depend on that "
"choice by running the selection without a start set."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The following items have fewer than two observed scores and cannot be "
"analyzed: %s. Remove them from the analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The following items have non-integer scores: %s. Mokken scale analysis works "
"with ordered item scores (0, 1, 2, ...). Check that these variables are item "
"scores and not scale means or other derived values."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The following items have zero variance in the cases analyzed (every "
"respondent gave the same score): %s. A constant item cannot order "
"respondents, so its scalability is undefined. Remove it from the analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The genetic algorithm does not use a start set; the start set was ignored. "
"Use Mokken's procedure to start the first scale from chosen items."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The genetic algorithm searches many candidate partitions at once, "
"recombining and mutating them across generations, and keeps the partition "
"with the largest scales that meet the criteria (Straat et al., 2013; see "
"references). It is less likely than Mokken's procedure to get trapped by an "
"early choice, at the price of computing time and some randomness; the seed "
"makes the result reproducible. Compare both: agreement strengthens the "
"conclusion."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The item order is estimated from the item means in this sample, which is the "
"usual choice when no ordering is hypothesized."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The item order is fixed as the order of the item list, as a hypothesis to "
"test (for example, items designed from easy to hard). Items listed first are "
"expected to be the most popular."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The item-rest correlation is the correlation of each item with the sum of "
"the other items. A low value marks an item that contributes little to the "
"total score."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The item-score proportions of each subject are averaged, so every subject "
"counts equally whatever its number of raters. This reproduces the original "
"estimator of Snijders (2001) and Koopman et al. (2020); with unequal group "
"sizes the weighted version is preferred."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The item-score proportions of each subject are weighted by the number of "
"raters, so subjects rated by many raters count more. This is mokken's "
"current default, which corrects a bias of averaged proportions when group "
"sizes differ (Koopman et al., 2022; see references)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The lower limit of the confidence interval of H (%s) is below .30, so this "
"sample does not rule out that the scale is unscalable. Interpret its "
"strength with caution, and prefer a test against .30 (Wald-based or "
"range-preserving) to the point estimate."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The lowest and highest scores observed in the data define the score range "
"used to reverse negatively worded items and to bound imputed scores. If an "
"extreme category was never chosen in this sample, declare the range instead, "
"so reversal maps each score to its true mirror."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The manifest scale cumulative probability model (MSCPM) compares the item "
"step response functions instead of the item means, so it checks a stronger "
"property: that every item step keeps its order (Ligtvoet et al., 2011; see "
"references). Use it when you need the ordering at the level of response "
"categories."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The Molenaar-Sijtsma reliability of the total score is %s. MS uses the "
"item-step response functions estimated under the double monotonicity model, "
"so it is most trustworthy when invariant item ordering holds (check it in "
"Model Assumptions). High reliability does not show that the total score "
"measures a single attribute: a sum of two correlated subscales can be very "
"reliable."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The monotone homogeneity model (one attribute, local independence, "
"monotonicity) justifies ordering respondents by their total score. The "
"double monotonicity model adds nonintersecting item step response functions, "
"which also justifies ordering the items in the same way for everyone "
"(invariant item ordering)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The norm statistics could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The order codes must be %d whole numbers, one per item, separated by commas "
"or spaces."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The order codes you typed fix the position of each item; equal codes form "
"clusters of tied items. Use it to test a hypothesized order that is not the "
"list order."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The original Z test (Mokken, 1971; see references) tests whether each "
"coefficient is larger than zero. It cannot test against a lower bound such "
"as .30 (the value %s you typed is used only by the other tests); choose the "
"Wald-based or range-preserving test to check whether coefficients exceed a "
"lower bound."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The original Z test was replaced by the Wald-based test, because it cannot "
"test Hi against c or account for nested data."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The outlier statistics could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The P-matrix method checks nonintersection with two matrices: P(++) holds "
"the proportion of respondents passing each pair of item steps, and P(--) the "
"proportion failing both."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The P-matrix method could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The procedure removes the item with the worst flags, then repeats the check "
"on the remaining items, until none is flagged. Being stepwise, it does not "
"guarantee the largest possible set without flagged items."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The range you declared defines how negatively worded items are reversed "
"(lowest + highest - score) and bounds imputed scores. Declare the range of "
"the response format, for example 0 to 4 for a five-point item scored from 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The range-preserving test compares log(1 - H) with log(1 - %s), using a "
"standard error on that scale, so it stays accurate near the ceiling (Koopman "
"et al., 2021; see references). Prefer it to the Wald-based test for strong "
"scales or small samples."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The rest-score method checks nonintersection of the item step response "
"functions, which the double monotonicity model requires: for any two items, "
"the curves of their item steps must not cross."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The rest-score method could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The scalability coefficient H compares the observed Guttman errors (a "
"respondent passing a harder item step but failing an easier one) with the "
"number expected if items were unrelated. Hij describes an item pair, Hi an "
"item and H the whole scale. Mokken's rule of thumb: a scale needs H and "
"every Hi of at least .30; .30 to .40 is weak, .40 to .50 moderate, .50 or "
"higher strong."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The scalability of this scale could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The scale H could not be estimated, so the scale cannot be evaluated. Check "
"the item scores and the messages in Notes."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The scale H is %s, below Mokken's minimum of .30. The items do not order "
"respondents consistently enough to be summed into one scale score: there are "
"almost as many Guttman errors as would be expected if the items were "
"unrelated. This usually happens when the items measure more than one "
"attribute or include items unrelated to the rest. Run Automated Item "
"Selection to see whether the items split into separate scales."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The scale H is %s: a %s. Taken together, the items order respondents more "
"consistently than unrelated items would. This is necessary but not "
"sufficient for using their sum score: a single H does not show that the "
"items measure one attribute (a set made of two strong subscales can still "
"reach it; check Automated Item Selection) nor that the model assumptions "
"hold (check Model Assumptions)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The selection formed %d scales with c = %s. The items order respondents "
"consistently within each group but not across groups, which suggests related "
"but distinct attributes, or method effects such as item wording."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The selection is exploratory and capitalizes on chance: with another sample "
"the borderline items may move. Confirm the partition in new data, with the "
"genetic algorithm, or with a sequence of lower bounds."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The selection was run for %d lower bounds from %s to %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The sequence of lower bounds is empty: From must be smaller than To. Correct "
"the values in Selection criterion."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The subject variable has fewer than two subjects. Two-level scalability "
"compares raters of different subjects; select the variable that identifies "
"the rated person or object."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"The Wald-based test divides the difference between each coefficient and the "
"lower bound %s by its standard error. A significant one-sided result means "
"the coefficient is larger than the bound, which is stronger evidence than a "
"point estimate above it (Koopman et al., 2022; see references)."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "The weights could not be computed: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"There is no agreed cut-off. Values near 1 mean the grouping hardly depends "
"on the sample; items that change scale in a large share of samples should be "
"interpreted, and reported, as uncertain. Bootstrap stability is not "
"replication: confirm the partition in new data."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid ""
"These items were reversed before the analysis (lowest + highest - score, as "
"mokken::recode()), so that a higher score means more of the attribute on "
"every item. Unreversed negatively worded items produce negative scalability "
"coefficients and break the scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/texts.R
msgid "This coefficient could not be estimated for these data."
msgstr ""

